WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:05.380
Welcome to Fascinating Frontiers, Episode 121 for July 4th, 2026.

00:00:06.220 --> 00:00:09.840
Let's look at what's new across the frontiers of space and science.

00:00:10.220 --> 00:00:16.160
A robotic spacecraft just launched to nudge a long-running NASA observatory back into a stable orbit.

00:00:16.840 --> 00:00:25.640
The Pegasus XL lifted off from Quajaline atoll, carrying Catalyst Space's Link Robotics servicer on the Swift Reboost mission.

00:00:26.400 --> 00:00:36.220
Stargazer released the rocket near 166 degrees west, 7.5 degrees north at roughly 0836 UTC on July 3.

00:00:37.100 --> 00:00:44.540
Link will rendezvous with NASA's Neil Garrals Swift Observatory and perform a controlled boost to raise its orbit.

00:00:45.220 --> 00:00:49.240
Communications with the servicer have already been established after separation.

00:00:49.840 --> 00:00:53.480
The flight may mark the final use of the Pegasus rocket family.

00:00:53.480 --> 00:01:00.000
Teams will next test Link's proximity operations and docking hardware before the reboost maneuver.

00:01:00.680 --> 00:01:06.980
Two privately built spacecraft performed a controlled rendezvous and intercept under the Victus Hayes mission.

00:01:07.840 --> 00:01:12.840
The exercise demonstrated rapid response orbital maneuvering for the U.S. Space Force.

00:01:13.780 --> 00:01:19.000
One vehicle approached and passed the other at close range while ground teams tracked both objects.

00:01:19.000 --> 00:01:24.260
The data will inform future tactics for on-orbit inspection and servicing.

00:01:24.780 --> 00:01:29.520
Now we turn from active orbital operations to how we discovered distant worlds.

00:01:30.220 --> 00:01:39.420
Archive data from NASA's Transiting Exoplanet Survey Satellite revealed a Jupiter mass world whose gravity briefly magnified light from a background star.

00:01:39.820 --> 00:01:46.180
The event marks the spacecraft's initial use of micro-lensing rather than its usual transit method.

00:01:46.180 --> 00:01:58.500
The planet designated HD-126,105B in related catalogue entries, orbits at 1.36 AU from its K-type host.

00:01:59.080 --> 00:02:06.020
The technique opens a new search channel for worlds that do not cross their stars from TSES's line of sight.

00:02:06.680 --> 00:02:11.540
Follow-up observations from ground telescopes are planned to refine the mass in orbit.

00:02:11.540 --> 00:02:24.600
If the sun were replaced by a bowling ball on a vast frozen lake, a passing star would bend the surrounding ice just enough to focus light from a distant object into a brief bright flash visible from shore.

00:02:25.220 --> 00:02:27.860
That flash is gravitational micro-lensing.

00:02:28.480 --> 00:02:36.800
The foreground mass warps space-time so that light rays from a background source converge toward the observer for a few hours or days.

00:02:37.800 --> 00:02:50.520
In the TSES case, the lens was a Jupiter mass planet roughly 1.4 AU from its host star, and the brightening lasted long enough for the satellite's cameras to record it in archived frames.

00:02:51.260 --> 00:03:02.740
The probability of catching such an alignment is tiny, roughly one in a million for any given background star, yet the survey's wide field and repeated visits made the first detection possible.

00:03:02.740 --> 00:03:14.040
The same physics that lets us weigh planets we cannot see also lets us measure the mass of free-floating worlds and even rogue black holes, provided the alignment is caught.

00:03:14.600 --> 00:03:22.440
The open question is whether the current archive already contains dozens more events that simply have not been searched with the right algorithms.

00:03:23.260 --> 00:03:29.000
Astronomers have identified a system in which six supermassive galaxies are simultaneously merging.

00:03:29.000 --> 00:03:34.940
The structure was found in deep imaging and spectroscopic data from several large telescopes.

00:03:35.680 --> 00:03:40.740
Each galaxy hosts a central black hole that is actively accreting during the encounter.

00:03:41.500 --> 00:03:46.540
The event offers a rare laboratory for studying how the most massive galaxies assembled.

00:03:47.360 --> 00:03:54.940
Additional multi-wave length observations are underway to measure gas inflows and star formation rates across the merging system.

00:03:54.940 --> 00:04:09.320
The James Webb Space Telescope has discovered M1149 hyphen BSG hyphen Z5, a barred spiral galaxy already in place when the universe was less than 1.2 billion years old.

00:04:09.920 --> 00:04:14.440
The bar and spiral arms appear well-developed despite the early epoch.

00:04:14.960 --> 00:04:19.740
The detection challenges models that predict slower formation of such ordered structures.

00:04:20.740 --> 00:04:25.840
Follow-up spectroscopy will measure the galaxy's stellar mass and star formation history.

00:04:26.500 --> 00:04:39.860
Galaxy SDSS J110546.07 plus 145202.4 has remained bright at radio wavelengths for several years after an initial outburst.

00:04:40.400 --> 00:04:47.660
The emission is thought to arise from material still interacting with the central black hole long after the original disruption event.

00:04:47.660 --> 00:04:54.140
The source is the first known example of such a prolonged radio transient at a galactic nucleus.

00:04:55.060 --> 00:04:59.860
Continued monitoring will test whether similar flares have been missed in earlier surveys.

00:05:00.180 --> 00:05:07.980
Extreme winds on several ultra-hot Jupiters have been mapped for the first time, revealing the presence of planetary magnetic fields.

00:05:08.460 --> 00:05:14.720
The measurements come from high-resolution spectroscopy of iron and other metals in the day-side atmospheres.

00:05:14.720 --> 00:05:19.260
Field strengths appear comparable to those inferred for Jupiter itself.

00:05:19.820 --> 00:05:26.380
The discovery supplies the first direct evidence that these close-in gas giants generate internal dynamos.

00:05:27.140 --> 00:05:33.880
Additional targets are now being observed to test how field strength varies with orbital distance and stellar irradiation.

00:05:35.000 --> 00:05:42.900
JWST observations have identified sodium chloride clouds in the atmosphere of the well-studied world, nicknamed the pink planet.

00:05:42.900 --> 00:05:47.900
The clouds form at altitudes where temperatures allow salt vapor to condense.

00:05:48.240 --> 00:05:53.440
This marks the first clear detection of chloride bearing clouds on any exoplanet.

00:05:54.080 --> 00:05:58.580
The finding helps refine models of cloud chemistry under intense stellar heating.

00:05:59.260 --> 00:06:03.880
Further spectra are expected to map how the cloud deck changes with longitude.

00:06:04.420 --> 00:06:11.960
A gas giant has been found orbiting the white dwarf remnant of a sun-like star that previously expanded into a red giant.

00:06:11.960 --> 00:06:18.160
The planet's orbit lies far enough out that it avoided engulfment during the star's expansion.

00:06:18.680 --> 00:06:25.100
Its survival supplies a direct analog for the possible fate of Jupiter once the sun leaves the main sequence.

00:06:25.880 --> 00:06:30.560
Additional systems are being searched to determine how common such survivors may be.

00:06:31.180 --> 00:06:36.560
The two planets reached closest approach at 1 a.m. Eastern daylight time on July 4.

00:06:36.560 --> 00:06:44.220
By roughly 4.30 a.m. local daylight time both worlds stood about 16 degrees above the Eastern horizon.

00:06:45.040 --> 00:06:51.180
Mars appeared as a bright red point while Uranus required binoculars or a small telescope to resolve.

00:06:52.140 --> 00:06:56.920
The conjunction offers sky watchers a convenient reference for locating the ice giant.

00:06:57.700 --> 00:07:01.360
Observers in the Americas and Europe had the best viewing geometry.

00:07:01.360 --> 00:07:07.820
A new Hubble portrait shows a bright red emission nebula threaded with clusters of blue and white stars.

00:07:08.320 --> 00:07:15.680
The image highlights regions where intense ultraviolet radiation from the hottest stars is ionizing surrounding hydrogen gas.

00:07:16.400 --> 00:07:20.480
The nebula lies several thousand light years from Earth in the Milky Way plane.

00:07:21.160 --> 00:07:26.380
The data will help calibrate models of feedback between massive stars and their nadal clouds.

00:07:27.380 --> 00:07:36.000
A stacked exposure totalling just over three hours on a C-star S50 smart telescope produced a high resolution view of the cocoon nebula.

00:07:36.820 --> 00:07:41.420
The image reveals the nebula's intricate dust lanes and embedded star forming regions.

00:07:42.280 --> 00:07:45.640
Processing was completed in standard photo editing software.

00:07:46.360 --> 00:07:52.360
Such compact instruments are enabling more backyard astronomers to contribute scientifically useful data.

00:07:52.360 --> 00:07:59.780
The telescope released a set of images highlighting red, white and blue features across the solar system and beyond.

00:08:00.500 --> 00:08:05.840
The release coincides with the national milestone and showcases ongoing science operations.

00:08:06.660 --> 00:08:10.460
Hubble continues to deliver data more than three decades after launch.

00:08:11.020 --> 00:08:17.480
Sophie Adonaut captured a striking green auroral ribbon from the International Space Station during the Epsilon mission.

00:08:17.480 --> 00:08:23.400
The time lapse covers more than an hour of orbital night and shows the curtain shifting and brightening.

00:08:23.940 --> 00:08:27.440
The footage was recorded on day 139 of the mission.

00:08:27.920 --> 00:08:32.940
It provides a high resolution record of auroral dynamics at the altitude of the station.

00:08:33.780 --> 00:08:39.760
The astronaut returned to her Massachusetts hometown after her recent space flight and met former classmates.

00:08:40.700 --> 00:08:45.680
The visit highlighted local pride in her contributions to NASA's commercial crew program.

00:08:45.680 --> 00:08:50.340
Williams has flown on multiple missions to the International Space Station.

00:08:50.800 --> 00:08:58.600
Keep an eye on the next round of JWST spectra that could map how those salt clouds shift across WASP 39B.

00:08:59.400 --> 00:09:01.880
That covers today's space and science news.

